<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Amritsar (1846)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1846">1846</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-anglo-sikh-war">First Anglo-Sikh War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of Amritsar of 1846 was an agreement by which the British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> sold <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulab-singh">Gulab Singh</a>, the ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jammu">Jammu</a>, for 7.5 million <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rupee">rupees</a>. It followed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-anglo-sikh-war">First Anglo-Sikh War</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-lahore">Treaty of Lahore</a>, which had stripped the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikh-empire">Sikh Empire</a> of Kashmir. Accounts of the Sikh Empire treat these settlements as stages in the empire's dismemberment.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a>'s decisive victory at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sobraon">Battle of Sobraon</a>, Raja <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulab-singh">Gulab Singh</a>, acting as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wazir">Wazir</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikh-empire">Sikh Empire</a>, negotiated peace terms that included the cession of the land between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sutlej-river">Sutlej</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beas">Beas</a>, an indemnity of 1.5 crore <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rupee">rupees</a> and a drastic reduction of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> army. The regent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jind-kaur">Rani Jindan</a> then replaced him with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lal-singh">Lal Singh</a>, who said the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a> court could pay only half a crore in cash and offered territory for the rest. The British asked for the whole hill country between the Beas and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a>, including the Kashmir Valley.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulab-singh">Gulab Singh</a> had built strong relations with the British, and the Governor-General offered to recognise him as independent sovereign of the ceded lands if he paid the indemnity. Article XII of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-lahore">Treaty of Lahore</a> bound the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> state to accept his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> over whatever territory the British granted him by a separate treaty. The British kept the land between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beas">Beas</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ravi">Ravi</a>, with the Kullu Valley and the forts of Kangra and Noorpur.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>The treaty was signed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amritsar">Amritsar</a> on 16 March 1846 by Frederick Currie and Brevet-Major Henry Montgomery Lawrence for the Governor-General, Sir Henry Hardinge, and by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulab-singh">Gulab Singh</a> in person. It gave him and his male heirs the hill country east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a> and west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ravi">Ravi</a>, including Chamba and excluding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahaul">Lahul</a>, for 75 lakhs of Nanak Shahi rupees. He was to refer disputes with his neighbours to British arbitration, join British troops in the hills with all his forces, and employ no European or American without consent. In token of British supremacy he was to send each year one horse, twelve shawl goats and three pairs of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a> shawls.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The treaty began <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dogras">Dogra</a> rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a>. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulab-singh">Gulab Singh</a>'s officials began collecting revenue in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hazara">Hazara</a> in May 1846, Pashtun and other groups resisted, and in early 1847 he returned Hazara to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a> court in exchange for land nearer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jammu">Jammu</a>. Henry Lawrence settled a dispute over Chamba the same year, leaving it independent of Kashmir under British suzerainty. Gulab Singh's descendants ruled the princely state of Jammu and Kashmir as maharajas until its accession to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in 1947.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Treaty of Amritsar (1846)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The First Anglo-Sikh War (1845–1846) ended in Sikh defeat at Sobraon; the Treaty of Lahore stripped the empire of the Jullundur Doab and Kashmir, which the British then sold to the Dogra Gulab Singh for 7.5 million rupees under the <b>Treaty of Amritsar (1846)</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sikh-empire">Sikh Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>After the First Anglo-Sikh War, the British sold him the Kashmir valley by the <b>Treaty of Amritsar</b> in 1846, creating a large state under the Dogra dynasty.</blockquote><a href="/state/jammu-and-kashmir-princely-state">Jammu and Kashmir (princely state)</a></li></ul></section>
